  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Investigate interior angles of regular $n$-gons.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p18-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the measure of each interior angle of a regular $n$-gon for values of $n$ from 3 to 10.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=3:60^{\\circ}$; $n=4:90^{\\circ}$; $n=5:108^{\\circ}$; $n=6:120^{\\circ}$; $n=7:\\frac{900}{7}^{\\circ}$; $n=8:135^{\\circ}$; $n=9:140^{\\circ}$; $n=10:144^{\\circ}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}I_3&=\\frac{(3-2)\\times180^{\\circ}}{3}=60^{\\circ}\\\\I_4&=\\frac{(4-2)\\times180^{\\circ}}{4}=90^{\\circ}\\\\I_5&=\\frac{(5-2)\\times180^{\\circ}}{5}=108^{\\circ}\\\\I_6&=\\frac{(6-2)\\times180^{\\circ}}{6}=120^{\\circ}\\\\I_7&=\\frac{(7-2)\\times180^{\\circ}}{7}=\\frac{900}{7}^{\\circ}\\\\I_8&=\\frac{(8-2)\\times180^{\\circ}}{8}=135^{\\circ}\\\\I_9&=\\frac{(9-2)\\times180^{\\circ}}{9}=140^{\\circ}\\\\I_{10}&=\\frac{(10-2)\\times180^{\\circ}}{10}=144^{\\circ}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Some of the values you obtained are whole numbers. Others are not. Find all values of $n$ for which the measure of each interior angle of a regular $n$-gon is an integer number of degrees.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p18-q11-b-short-values",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "n=3,4,5,6,8,9,10,12,15,18,20,24,30,36,40,45,60,72,90,120,180,360",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p18-q11-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "These are precisely the divisors of 360 that are at least 3.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}I&=\\frac{180(n-2)}{n}\\\\I&=180-\\frac{360}{n}\\end{align*}$$\nThe angle is an integer number of degrees exactly when $n$ divides 360. Since a polygon has at least three sides, the possible values are\n$$\\begin{gathered}3,4,5,6,8,9,10,12,15,18,20,\\\\24,30,36,40,45,60,72,90,120,180,360.\\end{gathered}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      }
    ]
  },
